  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Rodolfo Dur\u00e1n drove in three runs and the bullpen closed it without drama.",
    "what_this_means": "They got in front in the second and never looked back. Dur\u00e1n's homer made it 3-1, a comfortable margin they held through nine innings. Walker Buehler worked five solid innings, allowing one run on six hits with five strikeouts. Mason Miller threw 1.1 scoreless for the save, and the relievers in between. Rodriguez, Adam, Morejon. Didn't give Baltimore anything to work with. This was a team offense that got runners on and converted them. Dur\u00e1n was the finisher, but the lineup showed some teeth. They needed that look heading into a division that's still seven games away.",
    "what_to_watch": "They won by one run for the second straight game. That's not a pattern yet, but it's a note. The same formula works when everyone participates. A quality start, timely hitting, and a bullpen that executes. This works until it doesn't. They didn't have much room for error tonight, and they didn't need it. Three-RBI nights don't happen often enough to carry a contender alone. So the fact that the rest of the offense chipped in matters more than Dur\u00e1n's line. The pitching held. The bats delivered just enough. They are one-run wins away from something sustainable or something fragile.",
